Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.37,0:00:03.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Impose the budget on the\Ncrowd, during training.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.85,0:00:07.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Draw a batch of snapshots at\Nany {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}, even without data.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.25,0:00:12.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Average their energy change\Nand square it: this penalty,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.20,0:00:16.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the crowd loss, vanishes\Nonly when the crowd balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.08,0:00:18.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But there is a trap.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.72,0:00:22.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even for a balanced crowd,\Na batch's average wobbles;
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.39,0:00:25.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}its square grows with the scatter.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.29,0:00:27.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So training also squeezes the scatter,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.90,0:00:32.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}nudging every snapshot toward\Nbalance: toward the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:32.70,0:00:35.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It showed up while\Nbuilding this experiment.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.91,0:00:39.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The fix: split the batch\Ninto two independent
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.08,0:00:41.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}juries and multiply their averages.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.30,0:00:47.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, the scatter drops out,\Nleaving only the crowd's imbalance.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.78,0:00:51.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Does it pay off? The next tests will tell.
